---
import type { LayoutProps } from '@blocks/Layout/layout'
import Layout from '@blocks/Layout/Layout.astro'

const layout: LayoutProps = {
    seo: {
        title: 'Building Local Semantic Job Matching with ONNX + Transformers | Alex',
        url: 'https://Alex247Git.github.io/blog/semantic-job-matching/',
        description: 'How I built privacy-first, zero-cost semantic job matching using sentence-transformers running locally on ONNX Runtime — no API keys, no per-request costs.',
        faviconUrl: '/logo.svg'
    },
    menu: {
        logo: {
            html: '<svg xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" width="24" height="24" viewBox="0 0 24 24" fill="none" stroke="white" stroke-width="2" stroke-linecap="round" stroke-linejoin="round"><path d="m3 9 9-7 9 7v11a2 2 0 0 1-2 2H5a2 2 0 0 1-2-2z"/><polyline points="9 22 9 12 15 12 15 22"/></svg>'
        }
    },
    footer: {
        className: 'site-footer'
    }
}
---

<Layout {...layout}>
    <div slot="menu">
        <a href="/">Home</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/Alex247Git/jobsearch" target="_blank">JobSearch repo</a>
        <a href="https://github.com/Alex247Git" target="_blank">GitHub</a>
    </div>

    <article class="post">
        <p class="post-meta">Sprint notes · Node.js · Machine Learning · ~6 min read</p>
        <h1>Building local semantic job matching with ONNX + transformers</h1>

        <p class="lede">
            Most "AI job matching" features you see are a wrapper around an LLM API: send the
            resume, pay per token, hope the response is parseable. For my
            <a href="https://github.com/Alex247Git/jobsearch" target="_blank">JobSearch platform</a>,
            I wanted matching that runs <strong>locally</strong> — no API keys, no per-request
            costs, no user data leaving the server. Here's how it works.
        </p>

        <h2>The model: a 90MB sentence-transformer</h2>
        <p>
            I use <code>Xenova/all-MiniLM-L6-v2</code> through
            <code>@huggingface/transformers</code>, which runs the model on
            <strong>ONNX Runtime</strong> in-process with the Node.js API. It converts any text —
            a candidate profile, a job posting — into a 384-dimensional embedding vector where
            <em>distance means semantic similarity</em>. "Frontend developer with React" lands
            close to "UI engineer, 3 years of React experience", even though the strings share
            almost nothing.
        <h2>From profile to comparable vector</h2>
        <p>
            Raw DB rows aren't text models can reason about, so every record goes through a
            composition step that flattens structured fields into one consistent sentence:
        </p>
        <pre><code>Title: Senior Frontend Dev
Description: Build React dashboards...
Skills: React, TypeScript
Education: BSc Computer Science
Certifications: AWS CCP
Languages: English, Greek
Job Type: Full-time
Location: Athens
Salary: 45k
Remote Option: Hybrid</code></pre>
        <p>
            Both sides of the market — candidate profiles and job postings — use the same
            composition, then get embedded with <em>mean pooling</em> and L2 normalization in a
            single batched pass. Batch-embedding both collections at once mattered: it cut a
            full regeneration run from minutes to seconds.
        </p>

        <h2>Scoring: cosine similarity with a floor</h2>
        <p>
            Because the vectors are normalized, similarity is just a dot product — but raw
            cosine scores are hard to present: unrelated pairs don't score 0, they score
            0.1–0.3. So I apply a <strong>threshold-and-rescale</strong>:
        </p>
        <pre><code>function normalizeScore(score, threshold = 0.5) &#123;
    if (score &amp;lt; threshold) return 0;          // hard floor: unrelated pairs vanish
    const scaled = ((score - threshold) / (1 - threshold)) * 10;
    return Math.min(Math.max(scaled, 0), 10); // clamp to 0&#8211;10
&#125;</code></pre>
        <p>
            Below 0.5 cosine similarity → 0. Above it, the remaining band is stretched onto
            0–10. A smoke test with a frontend resume against a frontend job scores
            <strong>1.78/10</strong>, while an unrelated candidate scores <strong>0.15 → 0</strong>.
            The gap between "vaguely related" and "actually relevant" becomes visible to users.
        </p>

        <h2>The pipeline</h2>
        <pre><code>trigger (startup / profile update / daily 3AM / manual endpoint)
   → read candidates + jobs from MySQL
   → composeText() both sides
   → batched MiniLM embedding (local ONNX)
   → cosine similarity matrix
   → normalizeScore 0–10
   → upsert into `recommendations` (ON DUPLICATE KEY)
   → served via REST to both candidate and employer views</code></pre>
        <p>
            Regeneration runs at server startup, on profile changes, and daily at 3 AM. An
            admin can also force it with <code>POST /recommendations/generate</code>. Writes
            are idempotent upserts, so a crashed run can't poison the table — the next run
            simply overwrites it.
        </p>

        <h2>What bit me in production</h2>
        <ul>
            <li>
                <strong>Native dependencies.</strong> The transformers stack pulls in
                <code>sharp</code>/<code>onnxruntime-node</code>, whose prebuilt binaries caused
                npm audit findings. I pinned safe versions via package <code>overrides</code> —
                a good reminder that "pure JS" models still ship native code.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>DB pool exhaustion.</strong> Running job recommendations immediately
                followed by candidate recommendations starved the MySQL pool. A short
                settle-delay plus retry-with-backoff per pass fixed it — the candidate pass is
                marked non-critical and can fail without taking down the startup sequence.
            </li>
            <li>
                <strong>Model cold start.</strong> First inference loads ~90MB of weights. I
                initialize lazily on first use so the API doesn't block startup on it.
            </li>
        </ul>

        <h2>Was it worth it?</h2>
        <p>
            For this scale, absolutely: zero marginal cost per match, full data privacy, and
            deterministic behavior I can test. A 90MB local model covering the 80% case with
            zero running cost is hard to beat for a self-contained platform — the moment you
            need cross-lingual nuance or long-document reasoning, a hosted embedding API earns
            its price.
        </p>

        <p class="post-cta">
            The full implementation lives in the
            <a href="https://github.com/Alex247Git/jobsearch" target="_blank">JobSearch repo</a>
            — see <code>BackEnd/semanticMatcherNew.js</code> and
            <code>BackEnd/services/recommendationService.js</code>.
        </p>

        <p><a href="/" class="back-link">← Back to portfolio</a></p>
    </article>
</Layout>

